<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 650–<a href="/years/685">685</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Constantine IV Pogonatos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine IV (c. 650–685) was Byzantine emperor from 668 to 685. His reign saw the repulse of the Arab attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the 670s and the Third Council of Constantinople of 680–681, which condemned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monothelitism">Monothelitism</a>. His campaign of 680 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparuh</a> ended in defeat, and the peace of 681 recognised the new Bulgarian state south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-iv.jpg" alt="Constantine IV" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantine_IV_mosaic.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Constantine IV was the eldest son of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a> and Fausta, daughter of the patrician Valentinus. His year of birth is unknown, though often given as around 650. He was made co-emperor with his father in 654 and became emperor in September 668, when news arrived that Constans II had been assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><p>His first task was to suppress the revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, where conspirators had proclaimed an officer named Mizizios emperor. Pope Vitalian withheld recognition and the navy stayed loyal. Constantine landed at Syracuse, captured Mizizios and had him executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Constantine faced severe pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of Muʿāwiya I. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> captured Cyzicus and used it as a base, and their fleet took Smyrna and other coastal cities. Possession of the fleet allowed Constantine to defend the capital, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> gradually regained the initiative, with counteroffensives into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> that helped end fifteen years of continuous raids. Muʿāwiya's death in 680 closed this period. At home Constantine reintroduced the heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> follis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> to restore the currency.</p><p>In November 680 he convened the Sixth Ecumenical Council, also called the Third Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, which reaffirmed the doctrines of Chalcedon and ended the Monothelite controversy; it closed in September 681. In 680 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> under Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparukh</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Constantine besieged their camp in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> but, in poor health, left the army, which panicked and was defeated. In 681 he acknowledged the Bulgar state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> and agreed to pay tribute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> had been crowned with him, and the populace demanded that this stand, but in late 681 Constantine had their noses slit so that they would be ineligible to rule. He died of dysentery on 10 July 685, succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-ii">Justinian II</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a> venerates him as a saint for calling the council.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constantine IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 680 the emperor <b>Constantine IV</b> led an army and fleet against them, but his forces were routed as they withdrew, and the Bulgars crossed the Danube into the province of Moesia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=672" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Constantine IV"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 4064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 4064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=672">What was here in 672 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
